{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/app.py'","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Story library: the publish handler.\n\nEach story is stored in one DynamoDB item together with the three most similar\nstories already in the library (``similar``), so the reader page can show\n\"related\" without a query at read time.\n\"\"\"\nimport json\nimport logging\nimport re\nimport time\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"stories\")\n\nTABLE_NAME = \"stories\"\nTOP_N = 3\n\n_WORD = re.compile(r\"[a-z0-9]+\")\n\n\ndef words(text):\n    return set(_WORD.findall((text or \"\").lower()))\n\n\ndef similarity(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Shared-tag ratio (Jaccard), blended with shared words of the two bodies.\"\"\"\n    ta, tb = set(a.get(\"tags\", [])), set(b.get(\"tags\", []))\n    tag_score = len(ta & tb) / len(ta | tb) if (ta | tb) else 0\n    wa, wb = words(a.get(\"body\")), words(b.get(\"body\"))\n    word_score = len(wa & wb) / len(wa | wb) if (wa | wb) else 0\n    return round(0.7 * tag_score + 0.3 * word_score, 4)\n\n\ndef related(story, existing):\n    scored = []\n    for other in existing:\n        if other[\"slug\"] == story[\"slug\"]:\n            continue\n        scored.append({\"slug\": other[\"slug\"], \"score\": similarity(story, other)})\n    scored.sort(key=lambda s: s[\"score\"], reverse=True)\n    return scored[:TOP_N]\n\n\ndef validate(story):\n    for k in (\"slug\", \"title\", \"body\"):\n        if not story.get(k):\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing field: {k}\")\n    if not re.fullmatch(r\"[a-z0-9-]{3,80}\", story[\"slug\"]):\n        raise ValueError(\"bad slug\")\n\n\ndef publish(table, story):\n    \"\"\"Store ``story`` with its related stories. Returns the stored item.\"\"\"\n    validate(story)\n    existing = table.scan(TableName=TABLE_NAME).get(\"Items\", [])\n    item = {\n        \"slug\": story[\"slug\"],\n        \"title\": story[\"title\"],\n        \"tags\": sorted(set(story.get(\"tags\", []))),\n        \"body\": story[\"body\"],\n        \"published_at\": int(time.time()),\n        \"similar\": related(story, existing),\n    }\n    table.put_item(Item=item)\n    log.info(\"publish slug=%s similar=%d stored\", item[\"slug\"], len(item[\"similar\"]))\n    return item\n\n\ndef handler(event, table):\n    \"\"\"Lambda-style entry point for POST /stories. Body is the story as JSON.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        story = json.loads(event.get(\"body\") or \"{}\")\n        stored = publish(table, story)\n        return {\"statusCode\": 201, \"body\": json.dumps({\"slug\": stored[\"slug\"]})}\n    except ValueError as e:\n        return {\"statusCode\": 400, \"body\": json.dumps({\"error\": str(e)})}\n    except Exception:\n        log.exception(\"publish failed\")\n        return {\"statusCode\": 500, \"body\": json.dumps({\"error\": \"Internal Server Error\"})}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat notes/the-dynamodb-crash-that-only-appears-on-your-second-write.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ntitle: The DynamoDB crash that only appears on your second write\ndate: 2026-07-31\ntags: dynamodb, python, testing, aws-lambda, boto3\ntype: success\nreproducible: true\n---\n\n# The DynamoDB crash that only appears on your second write\n\nMy publish endpoint worked perfectly — once. The first story went in clean. The\nsecond one returned Internal Server Error, and the log said:\n\"TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.\"\n\nDynamoDB does not accept Python floats. Everyone learns this eventually; the\ninteresting part is why the first write survived. My API computes similarity\nscores between the incoming story and the existing ones, and stores the top\nmatches. On the very first publish the table was empty — no matches, no scores,\nno floats, no crash. The bug did not exist until the data did.\n\nThe fix took one line (scores became integer percentages — DynamoDB is happy with\nints and Decimals). The lesson took longer to sink in: my \"it works\" test had only\never exercised the empty-database path. Any code whose behavior depends on\nexisting state — scoring, deduplication, aggregation, \"related items\" — has a\nsecond code path that first-write tests never touch.\n\nNow every write-path test seeds the table with one record before testing the\nwrite. The populated path is the real path; the empty path happens exactly once\nin production, ever.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: any Python service writing to DynamoDB via boto3.\n\nYour agent will need from you: the location of your put_item / update_item calls,\nand your test setup.\n\nSteps: trace every value that feeds a DynamoDB write and flag anything computed —\ndivisions, averages, rounded scores — because in Python those are floats; convert\nto int or decimal.Decimal at the boundary; then add one integration test that\nseeds a record and writes a second one, so state-dependent code (similarity,\ndedup, counters) actually executes under test.\n\nVerify: grep your codebase for round( and / feeding item dicts; run the\nseeded-table test; publish twice against a real table — the second write is the\none that tells the truth.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
